PDC-OS IMPLEMENTED OS / KERNEL / CLI SUMMARY
============================================

Scope
-----
This describes code present in the current working tree, not roadmap items.
The LOC inventory at the end covers Rust source under src/ and NASM source
under arch/ only. Counts are physical lines (blank and comment lines included);
generated files, documentation, build scripts, and configuration are excluded.

OS and boot
-----------
PDC-OS is a freestanding, no_std, 64-bit x86-64 monolithic kernel. A Multiboot
v1 NASM bootstrap starts in 32-bit mode, checks PAE/long-mode support, creates
initial page tables, identity maps the first 64 MiB, optionally maps the
bootloader framebuffer, enables long mode and paging, and calls the Rust
kernel. It initializes COM1 early for bootstrap diagnostics. Rust initializes
CPU protection, display, memory, process/scheduler state, storage, interrupts,
services, filesystem, and terminal, then polls keyboard events while halting
between interrupts. The configured release/interface identity is PDC-OS
0.2.0 x86_64.

The linker script sets `_start` as the ELF entry and places the kernel at 1 MiB.
It separates executable text, read-only constants, and writable data/BSS
segments; unwind metadata is discarded because panics abort.

The Rust library is built for `x86_64-unknown-none` with `panic = "abort"` and
has no hosted standard library. The kernel uses fixed-capacity arrays and
bounded operations extensively. The main ownership/layout boundaries are:

- `arch/x86_64/`: Multiboot bootstrap and interrupt assembly.
- `src/arch/x86_64/`: CPU discovery/protection, address-space backend,
  x86-64 interrupt contexts, and TSS setup.
- `src/hal/`: architecture-neutral CPU, address-space, TLB, memory-protection,
  interrupt, timer, thread-context, and driver contracts.
- `src/kernel/`: generic-facing kernel core, interrupts, memory, processes,
  scheduler, synchronization, recovery, verification, security, and metrics.
- `src/drivers/`: port I/O, device registry, PIT, display, and ATA PIO.
- `src/fs/`: RAM filesystem, VFS, mount bookkeeping, and read cache.
- `src/userspace/`: terminal, keyboard, built-in command parser, syscalls,
  services, and fixed program catalog. The code remains privileged kernel code.
- `src/lib.rs`: boot orchestration, VGA/framebuffer text writer, print macros,
  and panic handler. `hardware` and `os` are compatibility re-exports.

The current boot and crate identity is 0.2.0. Interface and syscall ABI
versions are `0.2.0`; syscall numbers are assigned 1 through 23 and are
intended to remain stable within major version 0.

Boot-time sequence in the current kernel
----------------------------------------
The boot assembly requests a 1024x768x32 Multiboot RGB framebuffer, saves the
Multiboot registers, initializes a 64 KiB stack and COM1, checks required CPU
features, clears bootstrap tables, and builds 2 MiB identity mappings for the
first 64 MiB. If the Multiboot framebuffer fields are available, it maps two
adjacent 2 MiB pages at the 1 GiB alias. It then enables PAE, EFER.LME/NXE,
paging, and long mode; loads the GDT/data selectors; enables CR0.WP; and calls
the Rust entry point.

Rust boot stages perform the following work:

1. Start serial/recovery reporting and initialize CPU-0 per-CPU state.
2. Discover legacy device descriptors; initialize COM1 and detect CPU
   vendor/PAE/long-mode/NX capabilities.
3. Enable NXE when supported and verify CR0.WP. Initialize the framebuffer or
   use VGA text, then clear the display.
4. Parse the bootloader memory map and initialize frame allocation; initialize
   the TSS/IST stack and run the kernel invariant checks.
5. Add initial kernel task records, run architecture/process/synchronization/
   driver verification, initialize service and syscall state, and initialize
   the VFS (including best-effort ATA detection).
6. Create the startup shell process/task and read `/etc/inittab`; run the
   bounded security review.
7. Build/load the IDT, remap the legacy PIC, program the PIT, initialize PS/2,
   enable timer/keyboard IRQs, and enable interrupts.
8. Print the terminal banner and prompt; record a boot performance snapshot;
   print `PDC-OS boot complete`; then poll queued keyboard input and halt
   between interrupts.

Boot-stage names and typed device initialization errors are written to COM1.
Optional display, keyboard, and ATA failures are reported and fall back or
remain unavailable instead of preventing unrelated boot stages.

Implemented kernel and hardware
-------------------------------
- CPU discovery reports vendor, PAE, long mode, and NX support. The x86-64
  backend enables NXE when supported and CR0 write-protect, and sets up a GDT
  TSS with a double-fault IST stack.
- Physical memory uses the Multiboot memory map when available, reserves the
  first 16 MiB, and tracks 4 KiB frames in a bitmap covering up to 4 GiB. If
  no usable map is supplied, the allocator has a conservative 16-32 MiB
  fallback. It also provides a fixed 2 MiB kernel heap.
- Four-level x86-64 page tables support bounded address-space creation,
  mapping, unmapping, activation, TLB invalidation, and cleanup. Mapping checks
  enforce alignment, physical-address bounds, user/kernel address domains,
  and non-writable executable mappings; NX is applied to non-executable leaves.
- The IDT has exception/IRQ stubs, legacy 8259 PIC remapping/acknowledgement,
  and a PIT timer. Timer IRQs drive round-robin switching among executable
  kernel tasks using saved interrupt frames. The scheduler has 16 fixed task
  slots; process/thread bookkeeping has 16 process and 32 thread slots.
- PS/2 Set-1 input uses bounded initialization and a fixed IRQ event queue;
  decoding supports US and Dvorak layouts, modifiers, navigation, and keypad
  input. COM1 output is initialized and polled with bounded waits.
- Display output uses a bootloader-provided 32-bit RGB framebuffer when it
  satisfies the supported bounds, with a compact bitmap font; otherwise it
  falls back to VGA text. A device registry tracks descriptors, hardware
  resource overlaps, lifecycle state, and interrupt registrations.
- Primary-master ATA PIO can be identified and read by sector. The VFS mounts
  this device as a source when detected, but file listing/reading/writing is
  currently served by the in-memory filesystem, not an on-disk filesystem.
- Kernel diagnostics include boot-stage/error reporting, exception and panic
  context, halt/reboot/QEMU shutdown paths, allocation-free invariant checks,
  architecture verification, security checks, and atomic/TSC performance
  counters.

Architecture, memory, and execution details
-------------------------------------------
The generic-facing API uses `PhysicalAddress` and `VirtualAddress` wrappers.
Physical addresses must be nonzero, page-aligned, and below the allocator's
4 GiB domain. The documented virtual layout is:

- `0x0000000000000000`-`0x0000000000000fff`: null page, unavailable.
- `0x0000000000001000` to `0x0000800000000000`: user-address region.
- `0xffff800000000000` and above: kernel-address region.
- 4 KiB: allocation, mapping, and private page-table leaf unit.

The frame bitmap models 1,048,576 frames (4 GiB at 4 KiB each). The first
16 MiB is reserved. The Multiboot memory-map parser checks the boot info
address, advertised size, region arithmetic, and map bounds before marking
usable frames. If it discovers no usable frames, the fallback marks only
16-32 MiB usable. Counters track allocations, deallocations, failures, reclaims,
and active allocated bytes.

The kernel heap is a static 2 MiB region with an IRQ-safe lock, aligned
first-fit block selection, splitting, deallocation, coalescing, and fragment
statistics. Heap pointer/layout bounds are checked during deallocation.
Frame and heap allocation probes exercise repeated reuse and injected
exhaustion.

There are 16 address-space slots. Each address space owns its PML4 root and
private intermediate tables; mapping entries reference leaf frames, while
address-space destruction currently reclaims mapped leaf frames in addition to
private page-table frames.
Mapping validates alignment, address domain, page flags, and the no
writable-plus-executable policy. Non-executable leaf pages receive NX when
enabled. Unmapping clears a leaf and invalidates the page; activation writes
the root to CR3; destruction walks present private tables and returns owned
frames. A 2 MiB bootstrap mapping is not silently replaced by a 4 KiB mapping.

The ELF64 parser accepts bounded input (at most 4 MiB), validates ELF64
little-endian x86-64 headers and program-table ranges, then checks PT_LOAD
file/memory sizes, virtual address arithmetic, page rounding, and executable
entry coverage before copying segment data into mapped pages. It limits the
program-header count to 1024. It does not create an execution transition to
that entry point.

The separate kernel process/thread table has up to 16 process records and 32
thread records, with explicit Initializing/Running/Exiting/Reaped lifecycles.
Each created thread receives a 4 KiB kernel stack, a separately allocated
guard frame recorded in metadata, and a 4 KiB userspace stack mapping. The
guard frame is tracked but is not yet a virtual guard-page mapping. Cleanup
unmaps the user stack before releasing its physical frame, frees stack frames,
and destroys the process address space. The syscall layer also keeps a
separate fixed process table for its PID/UID/parent/entry/exit state.

The scheduler has 16 fixed task slots. Entry-bearing kernel tasks get a private
4 KiB stack and a synthetic 22-word x86-64 interrupt/IRET frame. On timer IRQ,
the interrupt frame for the current task (or bootstrap context) is saved and
the next Ready executable task frame is copied into place. Metadata-only task
records do not execute as independent programs. The scheduler supports
Ready/Running/Blocked states, explicit block/wake/cleanup operations, task
snapshots, and switch timing.

The per-CPU abstraction currently initializes CPU 0 and records CPU ID,
current-thread ID, interrupt nesting depth, and whether execution is in an
interrupt. It is not evidence of SMP support: the two-vCPU profile is
experimental/boot-only, and scheduling remains BSP/single-CPU.

Interrupts and synchronization
------------------------------
The assembly file generates normalized stubs for vectors 0-63 and vector 128,
preserves general registers, passes vector/error/CR2/frame data to Rust, and
restores the selected frame with `iretq`. CPU-error-code vectors use a
different stub shape. The IDT has 256 entries; vector 8 selects TSS IST1, and
vector 128 is the syscall gate. At present, unspecified IDT entries reuse the
timer stub.

The PIC is remapped to vectors 32-47. The active masks enable IRQ0 and IRQ1;
the timer advances an atomic tick counter and asks the scheduler to switch
frames, while keyboard IRQ1 reads and queues a PS/2 event. The PIC receives
EOIs, including checks for spurious IRQ7/IRQ15. Other IRQs are acknowledged
but have no general device-event pipeline. CPU exceptions report their name,
error code, and register context; page faults additionally report CR2. The
exception path disables interrupts and halts.

`IrqLock<T>` records the caller's RFLAGS, disables interrupts before acquiring
its atomic spin lock, and restores the original interrupt state when the guard
drops. `try_lock` does not spin on contention and is intended for bounded IRQ
work. The lock exposes acquisition/contention counters. The fixed 16-entry
`WaitQueue` rejects task 0, duplicates, and overflow and supports remove,
wake-one, wake-all, membership, length, and reason queries. Scheduler block and
wake methods are separate operations; the queue is not yet a full sleeping
mutex, I/O wait, or syscall wait implementation.

Drivers, display, keyboard, and storage
--------------------------------------
The 16-slot device registry stores typed descriptors and up to four resources
per device. Descriptors identify a device class and claimed I/O-port, IRQ, or
memory ranges. Overlapping claims are rejected; devices progress through
discovered/registered/initialized/active states, while the current normal path
uses discovered/initialized/active; removal clears descriptors and owned
interrupt registrations. IRQ registrations carry owner, vector,
PIC line, sharing mode, and an optional handler. Legacy discovery describes
COM1, PIT, PIC, PS/2, and VGA devices.

The display prefers an available Multiboot linear RGB 32-bit framebuffer and
stores text cells in a fixed buffer. It renders a 6x8 bitmap glyph cell and
adapts the text grid to display dimensions, capped at 170 columns by 128 rows.
Unsupported mode, pixel format, address, pitch, or size falls back to the
80x25 VGA text buffer. Text output handles CR/LF, tabs, wrapping, backspace,
scrolling, color, and a 512-line scrollback ring. The VGA writer and COM1
writer are separate IRQ-locked output sinks; COM1 is configured for 8-N-1 and
disables itself after a bounded transmit timeout.

The PS/2 driver bounds controller self-test/reset/scanning waits. Its IRQ path
reads one scancode, handles device status, decodes a fixed Set-1 lookup table,
and enqueues one of 64 key events. It tracks shift/control/alt/caps/num state,
US and Dvorak layouts, extended navigation keys, and keypad navigation/digits.
Queue overflow is counted and reported by the normal polling path. The
terminal handles queued events outside IRQ context.

ATA PIO uses primary-master IDENTIFY and a bounded status poll; its read path
transfers one 512-byte sector using 28-bit LBA. It has no partition parser or
filesystem implementation. The VFS can record `/mnt/ata` as a mount source if
the device is identified, but accesses below non-RAM mount points currently
return `NotMounted`.

Processes, system calls, and security boundary
----------------------------------------------
Fixed-size process and thread records track parent, UID, address-space, stack,
and lifecycle data. Thread creation allocates kernel stacks and mapped user
stack pages. The timer scheduler can switch kernel execution contexts.

The int 0x80 interface has a versioned set of 23 syscall numbers covering
console write, process identity/exit/fork/wait, ticks and frame allocation,
ELF loading, file handles, IPC, signals, UIDs, shared memory, and foreground
console ownership. Implemented operations use negative errno-style results;
pointer-shaped arguments get overflow and canonical-range checks. The ELF64
loader validates bounded ELF/program-header/segment ranges, maps PT_LOAD
segments, and replaces the current process address space.

Important current limitation: there is no ring-3 entry/return path or
per-process enforcement of user mappings. The shell, syscall dispatcher, and
kernel services execute in ring 0. Process and syscall features are therefore
an in-kernel prototype, not an isolated userspace environment; ELF loading
does not launch untrusted code at ring 3. `fork`, `wait`, ordinary file
handles, and registered shell programs have prototype/fixed-service semantics,
not a complete hosted process model.

Syscall numbers and implemented behavior
----------------------------------------
The `int 0x80` path uses RAX for the number, RBX/RCX/RDX for arguments, and
returns the result in RAX. The assembly gate is present in the IDT with a
user-callable gate descriptor, but no Ring 3 process entry path is installed.
The current dispatcher runs in ring 0. `dispatch_args` handles these numbers:

| Number | Call | Current behavior |
| ---: | --- | --- |
| 1 | `write` | Validates range/foreground ownership and writes to the console. |
| 2 | `exit` | Marks the current syscall process exited and cleans scheduler/process state. |
| 3 | `getpid` | Returns the current syscall-table PID. |
| 4 | `getticks` | Returns the PIT tick count. |
| 5 | `alloc_frame` | Allocates one physical frame and returns its address. |
| 6 | `fork` | Creates a new PID and empty address space; does not copy parent memory. |
| 7 | `wait` | Checks/reaps an exited child; it does not block waiting. |
| 8 | `exec_elf` | Validates/loads ELF segments into a new address space for the current PID and returns the entry address; it does not jump to it. |
| 9 | `open` | Opens an existing VFS path or `/dev/console` in a fixed handle table. |
| 10 | `read` | Reads a VFS file from the beginning or drains foreground console keys. |
| 11 | `close` | Closes a handle owned by the current PID. |
| 12 | `ipc_send` | Sends a bounded message to a known process, with non-root UID restrictions. |
| 13 | `ipc_recv` | Copies the next queued message for the current PID. |
| 14 | `signal_send` | Sets a bounded pending signal after UID checks. |
| 15 | `signal_recv` | Takes the next pending signal for the current PID. |
| 16 | `getuid` | Returns current UID. |
| 17 | `setuid` | Root may select a registered UID; non-root returns permission failure. |
| 18 | `shm_create` | Creates/looks up a bounded shared-resource key and mode. |
| 19 | `shm_attach` | Checks access and returns the shared-resource key. |
| 20 | `shm_write` | Writes up to 512 bytes subject to resource permissions. |
| 21 | `shm_read` | Reads a permitted resource into a validated range. |
| 22 | `console_foreground_set` | Assigns console foreground to a running PID. |
| 23 | `console_foreground_get` | Returns the current foreground PID. |

Unknown calls return `ENOSYS`; errors use negative errno-style values.
Pointer endpoints use checked arithmetic and must remain inside the canonical
user range (`0x1000` to below `0x0000800000000000`). This validates numerical
shape only: because execution stays in ring 0 and the kernel does not verify
that the range is mapped for the calling process, it is not safe against an
arbitrary untrusted caller.

`open` keeps 16 handles, associates each with a PID, and allocates descriptors
starting at 3. Console input is available through fd 0 or `/dev/console`;
`write` takes a buffer pointer and length (not a descriptor) and requires
foreground ownership. File operations are read-only through this ABI; the VFS
itself has kernel-level create/write methods. The legacy one-argument
`dispatch` wrapper does not implement the full ABI and returns `ENOSYS` for
several calls; the interrupt path calls `dispatch_args`.

Kernel services
---------------
The service subsystem is a fixed `IrqLock`-protected table. It supports up to
16 user identities (initialized with UID 0 `root`), 64 queued IPC messages
with 256-byte payloads, 16 shared resources with 512-byte payloads, and
pending signal bitmasks for PIDs 1-16. Non-root IPC is limited to matching
UIDs. Signals reject invalid numbers and unauthorized cross-UID sends.
Shared-resource read/write permissions recognize root, owner, and Unix-like
mode bits. Snapshots feed the `users` and `shm` shell displays.

Filesystem and shell
--------------------
The VFS is fixed-capacity and allocation-free: a RAM root filesystem has up
to 16 nodes, files hold up to 512 bytes, paths are limited to 32 bytes, and
there are four mount slots. It provides directory listing, file read/create/
write, mount bookkeeping, path validation that rejects traversal, and a
four-entry read cache. Initial content includes `/README`, `/hello.txt`,
`/etc/os-release`, `/etc/inittab`, `/bin/pdc-sh`, and `/sbin/init`. Standard
`/bin`, `/sbin`, `/etc`, and `/lib` directories are created. The shell resolves
relative paths from `/`.

RAMFS supports direct-child listing, bounded reads, file creation, and
whole-file writes; each file stores at most 512 bytes. The VFS has four mount
records and routes the root mount to RAMFS; non-RAM mount operations are
bookkeeping only. The path-normalization helper rejects empty, relative,
overlong, NUL-containing, and `..` paths, though the VFS entry points
themselves mostly use exact stored-path matching rather than applying a
normalization pass on every operation. A four-entry read-through cache copies
whole file contents and invalidates matching entries after writes; cache hit
and miss counts appear in performance diagnostics.

The interactive `pdc-os>` terminal has a 256-byte line buffer and
eight-command history. It supports character insertion, cursor movement,
Backspace/Delete, Home/End, Up/Down history recall, Page Up/Down scrollback
(512 lines), Ctrl+C, Ctrl+L, colored output, and a framebuffer/VGA fallback.
The command parser splits on whitespace; it has no quoting, shell expansion,
redirection, or command pipeline.
Built-in commands are:

  help, clear, history, display, echo, uname, uptime, pwd,
  perf, security, bench, mem, free, ps, ls, cat, head, wc,
  mount, pid, id, users, shm, bin, run, install, halt, reboot, shutdown

These are shell built-ins, not independently loaded executables. `run` looks
up one of 12 fixed program names and creates a process record; it does not
execute a separate binary. `install` copies a VFS file under `/bin`, `/sbin`,
or `/lib`. `bench` performs 32 bounded RAM-filesystem reads. `security` runs
the bounded adversarial checks. `perf` reports scheduler, IRQ, syscall,
cache, I/O, frame, page-table, and heap counters.

The fixed program catalog is `echo`, `uname`, `uptime`, `mem`, `free`, `ps`,
`pid`, `id`, `users`, `shm`, `ls`, and `cat`; `bin` lists their path and
description. The startup service parses the in-memory `/etc/inittab` and
reports recognized `/sbin/init` and `/bin/pdc-sh` entries. The registered
shell task's entry currently halts; interactive line editing and command
dispatch are driven by the kernel boot loop, not by an independent user-mode
shell process.

Command behavior:

- `help`, `clear`, `history`, `display`: command list, terminal reset/history,
  and current display mode/dimensions.
- `echo`, `uname`, `uptime`, `pwd`: print arguments, OS/version/architecture,
  PIT tick count, and the fixed root working directory.
- `perf`, `security`, `bench`: display counters, run bounded adversarial
  checks, and execute 32 RAMFS reads plus basic tick/task reporting.
- `mem`, `free`, `ps`, `pid`, `id`, `users`, `shm`, `bin`: show allocator,
  scheduler, current process/identity, registered users/resources, and
  program-catalog snapshots.
- `ls [PATH]`, `cat FILE`, `head FILE`, `wc FILE`, `mount [SOURCE TARGET]`:
  operate on fixed VFS/mount data. `head` prints up to 10 lines; `wc` counts
  newline bytes and total bytes.
- `run PROG` creates a process record for a catalog entry but doesn't execute
  a separate program image. `install SRC DEST` copies a VFS file to an allowed
  system directory, subject to fixed VFS limits.
- `halt`, `reboot`, `shutdown`: enter the halt path or request PC/QEMU reset
  or ACPI-style poweroff, with diagnostic fallback if the operation fails.

Security, recovery, and performance
-----------------------------------
The security review is run at boot and again by `security`. It uses bounded
checks for CPU NX/WP state, mapping address/permission rules, unknown syscalls,
invalid syscall pointers/lengths, malformed/oversized ELF input, VFS path
validation, oversized file writes, invalid mounts, and UID boundaries. Failures
are counted and individually reported on serial. The latest checked-in
`build/qemu-smoke.log` reports 19/19 review cases passed.

The boot architecture verification exercises interface/version metadata,
typed addresses, frame and heap reuse/exhaustion, malformed boot-info bounds,
address-space create/map/destroy, process/thread lifecycle and stack ownership,
IRQ-lock state restoration/try-lock behavior, wait-queue capacity and wake
operations, scheduler transitions, driver resource/IRQ lifecycle, TSS/per-CPU
initialization, and invalid process cleanup. Kernel invariants separately
check allocator counts/frame size, bounded scheduler/process/thread counts,
per-CPU initialization, and interrupt state. The latest checked-in
`build/qemu-smoke.log` reports 156/156 architecture cases and 7/7 invariant
checks with zero failures.
That log also records one baseline QEMU boot with NXE and CR0.WP enabled,
TSS.IST ready, PS/2 initialized, and primary-master ATA absent; these are
observations from that run, not universal hardware guarantees.

Recovery records boot stage and typed errors and can print CR2, CR3, RFLAGS,
ticks, task state, and memory use on exceptions/panics. Reboot uses the
emulated PC reset port with a keyboard-controller fallback; shutdown writes
the QEMU-compatible ACPI poweroff value; both end in a diagnostic halt if the
hardware request does not complete.

Performance counters use atomics and RDTSC cycle deltas for context switches,
IRQ handling, syscalls, COM1/VGA output, and cache activity. Memory counters
cover frame churn, page-table switches, heap allocations/deallocations, and
free-list fragments. TSC values are platform cycle counts, not calibrated wall
time. `make benchmark` runs a smoke boot and prints the serial `Performance:`
line.

Architecture contracts and current coverage
-------------------------------------------
The documentation now includes an architecture baseline, memory layout,
subsystem dependency map, initialization/ownership/error contracts, an HAL
contract, an ownership matrix, and synchronization/lock-order rules. It
describes the intended boundary: generic code should use HAL traits; subsystems
own their tables and cleanup; locks must be short and IRQ-safe; IRQ handlers
must not block or allocate; partial initialization and cleanup failures must
be explicit.

The source has the corresponding modules and several HAL traits. The current
x86-64 backend implements CPU discovery, address-space operations, TLB
invalidation, and CPU memory protection. The interrupt controller and timer
traits are declared, but IDT/PIC/PIT implementation remains in
`kernel/interrupts.rs` and `drivers`; it is not a complete trait-backed
architecture driver. Similarly, `memory.rs` and `interrupts.rs` still contain
x86 CR3/TLB or PIC port operations. The architecture layering exists, but the
hardware boundary is not yet enforced across every generic-looking module.

Recent 0.2.0 implementation work present in the tree
---------------------------------------------------
Compared with the prior 0.1.0 summary/source layout, the current tree includes
these implemented changes:

- Package and kernel interface identity advanced to 0.2.0, with stable
  versioned syscall metadata and a centralized OS identity used by the shell,
  VFS release file, and boot diagnostics.
- Code was reorganized under `arch`, `hal`, `kernel`, `drivers`, `fs`, and
  `userspace`; compatibility `hardware` and `os` paths remain as re-exports.
- The x86-64 backend gained typed address-space/TLB/protection operations,
  CPU feature state, NXE/CR0.WP readback, interrupt-context construction,
  TSS/IST setup, and CPU-0 per-CPU data.
- Process/thread ownership was added separately from scheduler task metadata:
  lifecycle states, address-space references, private stacks, user stack
  mappings, state transitions, and explicit cleanup.
- Synchronization gained the fixed wait-queue API, nonblocking IRQ try-lock,
  lock counters, and verification of interrupt-state preservation and queue
  limits.
- Driver support gained typed descriptors, resource claims/overlap checks,
  lifecycle states, IRQ registrations, and explicit removal.
- Boot verification was expanded to cover failure paths and cleanup; security
  checks and smoke-test expectations now require counted zero-failure reports.
- Architecture, subsystem-dependency, ownership, HAL, and synchronization
  documentation was added/expanded to match the code boundaries and known
  limitations.
- `Notes/V0.2.0_Goals` records the architecture-upgrade and documentation
  checklist as complete except for publishing the baseline, which remains a
  user-controlled release action rather than an implemented kernel feature.

Build, boot testing, and supported profiles
-------------------------------------------
The Makefile builds Rust core/compiler_builtins for `x86_64-unknown-none`,
assembles the two NASM files, links them with GNU `ld` into
`build/pdc-os.kernel`, and packages a GRUB ISO using xorriso. `make doctor`
checks host prerequisites; `make build` builds the ELF; `make run` starts
graphical QEMU; `make test` performs a bounded headless serial smoke boot.
The smoke script checks boot-stage, hardware, display, memory, security,
verification, performance, and completion markers; rejects nonzero
verification failures and panic/exception markers; and requires QEMU to remain
alive through the observation timeout.

Named QEMU profiles are baseline (256 MiB/1 vCPU), SMP-shaped (2 vCPU),
128 MiB, 256 MiB, 1 GiB, ATA with a temporary raw image, PS/2, serial-only,
and VGA. SMP is not parallel kernel execution; ATA is experimental detection
and raw-sector-read support, not an installer. The operations documentation
limits support claims to QEMU configurations and explicitly disclaims general
physical-hardware certification.

`make release` builds a versioned offline bundle with kernel ELF, ISO, GRUB
configuration, checksums, toolchain/source-revision manifest, all profile
smoke logs, and a fixed-epoch tarball. `make verify-release` validates
checksums and boot markers, rebuilds cleanly, compares ELF bytes exactly,
checks ISO structure, and reruns smoke boot. ISO UUID/catalog bytes may vary
with GRUB/xorriso even when boot behavior is sound.

Documentation/source alignment notes
-------------------------------------
The current assembly header and smoke-test marker request/expect 1024x768x32,
and the latest boot log reports a 1024x768 framebuffer. `boot/grub/grub.cfg`
and parts of `Notes/operations.md` still state 512x512. The framebuffer driver
adapts to the mode actually supplied, so its current dimensions should be
read from boot output or the `display` command.

`docs/memory-layout.md` describes the 64 MiB bootstrap identity map as starting
at 4 MiB; `arch/x86_64/boot/boot.asm` actually identity-maps physical addresses
0 through 64 MiB. The source and test log are the basis for this summary.

